Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.359
Okay, Jason from Maryland, appreciate the
phone call.

00:00:01.359 --> 00:00:02.359
Thank you so much.

00:00:02.359 --> 00:00:03.539
Let's go next to, where do we want to go next?

00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:10.070
Why don't we go to, um, let's go to our caller
from the six, seven, eight area code.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.269
Who's calling today from six, seven, eight

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:13.269
[inaudible]

00:00:13.269 --> 00:00:16.030
Oh, that's me, Mo.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.780
Hey ma, what's going on?

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:19.780
[inaudible]

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.080
he's doing well.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.210
So my question is kind of the same as the
guy that called them before.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.369
And it's about, I feel like a lot of people
who follow alternative media have this, uh,

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.320
we underestimate the power of mainstream media.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.610
And cause I noticed that with my friends and
family, a lot of us, a lot of them have their

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:46.030
perspective and perception of politics completely
determined by what is stated on popular news

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:52.420
and that be so basically what I was thinking
about is that most people do not consume a

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.059
news intelligently and they're just passive
viewers of news.

00:00:57.059 --> 00:00:58.840
So it's just background.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.200
My best background noise.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.330
So I would just wondering that because of
all this misinformation that when I talk with

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:14.180
my friends about politics, I noticed that
the discussion is very minimum and they tend

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.820
to use a lot of talking points to handle issues.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.900
And I feel like they're kind of useless agents
of the status quo because the talking points

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.979
you here don't really dig any deep onto topics.

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:27.640
There's no nuance in discussion.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:37.439
So I would just a wondering that you feel
that like since you have a big following that

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:43.369
you could mobilize your base to have people
come out and be go for like news reform kind

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.939
of like you have people who are going out
and advocate for electoral reform.

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:49.440
Yeah, I mean, listen, I am trying to do this.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.350
So like earlier this week when we did a story
about the testimony of the us Envoy to Ukraine

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.700
and it just, it's an argument over whether
what the guy says is credible.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.930
I took two thirds of the segment and I said,
you know, let's understand what, what different

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.959
people mean when they say there's no evidence
of a quid pro quo.

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:15.200
And trying to explain that the Republicans
are using the word evidence in a different

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.959
way than what it actually means.

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:20.250
And unless you understand this stuff and configure
it out for yourself, you're sort of going

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.970
to be reduced to only pick a talking point
that you like, which is basically what I hear

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:25.970
you saying.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.160
So there's a couple of different issues here.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:34.020
One is we can't really expect people to have
a more nuanced and deep understanding of an

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:39.010
issue if all of the inputs they get are equally
shallow.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.780
Corporate media gives them shallow inputs.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.890
Politicians often have an equally shallow
understanding of the issues.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.959
So unless someone understands how to get a
deeper understanding of issues, again, by

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:55.690
learning critical thinking and epistemology,
then by actually reading in depth about some

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:01.099
of these issues, it's unreasonable to expect
the average person to have a deeper understanding

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:03.650
than all of the inputs that they are being
informed by.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.349
So that sort of number one, you got to an
interesting point at the beginning though,

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:12.019
which I want to address, which is that there
are people who are in the independent online

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:17.900
media space who don't understand the power
that corporate media still has.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.810
And you see, you know where you see that really
clearly when you hear from an Andrew Yang

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:30.959
supporter or a Tulsi supporter who say, I
don't think any of these polls are right.

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:35.030
Don't people realize how much support these
people have online?

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.060
They are going to win the nomination.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.900
And it's like, no, no, no, no.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:46.170
You are in an enclave where it seems like
Tulsi Gabbert has huge support or Andrew Yang

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:51.120
has huge support and yet they keep polling
between zero and three and it's not an attack

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.540
on the candidates.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:58.030
It's just that a lot of people seem to be
missing that most Americans are busy enough

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.540
with their lives that as you say, they have
corporate news on often in the background,

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:07.190
they are not even considering candidates other
than the top candidates.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:09.400
I'm not praising that, I'm just pointing it
out.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.190
And I think that there is a disconnect that
is taking place and people aren't understanding

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:17.919
that most people's views are still primarily
informed by the talking points that you get

00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:23.059
on mainstream corporate media and you, and
you seem to be identifying that Mo

00:04:23.059 --> 00:04:24.059
[inaudible].

00:04:24.059 --> 00:04:28.020
Yeah, and I noticed that cause when I talk
to people about Andrew gang and other politicians,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.759
I mean other candidates that are running that
aren't as mainstream.

00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:35.909
There really isn't that much recognition or
knowing who these candidates are.

00:04:35.909 --> 00:04:39.270
And so I feel like a lot of people, especially
when they vote, they fall for it.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:43.720
It narrative of voting for who was electable
and who could be Trump versus actually looking

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.430
at their policy and having a real opinion
that they can support.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.810
And then one last thing I want to [inaudible]
I mentioned is whenever I talk about policy

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:55.110
with people, and I mentioned hoping that might
raise taxes or involved government intervention.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:00.270
As soon as people hear that they think socialism
and then they tend to react to strongly a

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.729
negative because they don't wait, they're
not really thinking deeper.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:09.809
So I realized that until we can have conversation
and news media can just present information

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:14.559
without a spin involved, most people are just
going to say, Oh, I've heard a lot of negative

00:05:14.559 --> 00:05:15.559
things about socialism.

00:05:15.559 --> 00:05:18.580
The media does a good job of, you know, creating
that negative association.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.689
So when people here to work, they just react
strongly without actually really thinking

00:05:21.689 --> 00:05:22.689
about it.

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:23.689
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.340
I mean a lot of people who have an opinion
about socialism don't even know what it is.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:31.759
And you can be against socialism and be informed
or be against it and be uninformed.

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:35.840
And obviously we're much better off if people
are informed in their opinions.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.469
Mo, I appreciate the phone call.

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:38.509
Thank you so much.

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:39.509
Points.

00:05:39.509 --> 00:05:40.659
Uh, all, uh, all well-made.

